ms that  include the expertise of intensive care  physicians so maybe even clinical ethics  committees can support in setting up  these processes  I think the most important thing is as  thing is that doctors not there only the  individual doctor is not in charge for  the whole responsibility taking in these  decisions that's one key issue of course  but what about normal doctors say in the  community village doctors who have  covered patients do they also or might  they also face these ethical dilemmas  and I think not those that dissing the  decision on who gets on the ventilator  is obviously not the primary care  physician but they have other triage  decisions if there are many patients who  want to transfer to the hospitals I if  there are many patients in need so I  think the same criteria of seriousness  and and comorbidities that go into the  triage protocols in the hospitals can  also plan be applied in the periphery  for the normal primary care physician or  will be at least of importance for him  or her  dr. Coates coming back to you how well  can you predict actually whether a  person will probably die even if treated  isn't that also a bit of a lottery there  but there are certain physiologic  parameters medical history appeared with  the medical experience of the treating  physician that will help to support your  decision but there's no certainty that  the to be made decision is T is the  right one but also there's a learning  curve as we know from Italy in Spain  that the teams make different decisions  depending on the situation and learning  however I'm very convinced automatic  teams will do it very best to really do  this triage to the best of their  knowledge  professor Winkler coming back to you as  an ethicist what can you tell a  front-line doctor who's facing a  decision like that could you give us an  example well  think first it would be important to to  acknowledge that these are tough  decisions that are kind of at odds with  our common 